Falsifies `kernel::sched::tests::an_interrupt_raised_while_its_line_is_masked_is_delivered_at_the_ack`.

Architecture: riscv64

The defect is an ACK that forgets its claim: `arch::irq::enable` drops the claimed context and
enables the source instead of completing it. The PLIC's gateway then waits for a completion that
never comes, so the source stays claimed and nothing it raises again is delivered. It is the same
outcome as a completion written where the PLIC ignores it, which QEMU cannot show directly because
its PLIC accepts any completion.

Expected to fail: the second assertion, "an interrupt raised while its line was masked was never
delivered after the ACK unmasked it", because the second raise of the console UART's
transmit-empty line is never routed.

riscv64 only. aarch64's GIC and x86_64's self-IPI have no claim to forget, and this file is
riscv64's, which neither of their kernels compiles.

diff --git a/kernel/src/arch/riscv64/irq.rs b/kernel/src/arch/riscv64/irq.rs
index 0342c41d0..6a5dcaf33 100644
--- a/kernel/src/arch/riscv64/irq.rs
+++ b/kernel/src/arch/riscv64/irq.rs
@@ -116,10 +116,8 @@ pub(crate) fn hold_claim(source: u32, context: usize) {
 /// With a claim outstanding, completes it on the claiming context, so the gateway forwards the next
 /// interrupt. Otherwise enables the source at its stable target hart (see [`target_context`]).
 pub fn enable(intid: u32) {
-    match CLAIM_CTX[intid as usize % MAX_SOURCES].swap(CTX_UNASSIGNED, Ordering::Acquire) {
-        CTX_UNASSIGNED => crate::drivers::plic::enable(intid, target_context(intid)),
-        claimed => crate::drivers::plic::complete(intid, claimed as usize),
-    }
+    let _ = CLAIM_CTX[intid as usize % MAX_SOURCES].swap(CTX_UNASSIGNED, Ordering::Acquire);
+    crate::drivers::plic::enable(intid, target_context(intid));
 }
 
 /// Reserve a vector a PCI function may deliver an MSI-X message on: `None` on this architecture.
